{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795430630,"nonce":"ytBmk3rKDy8Sy0PuVu85cnff4Gk=","title":"The iOS runtime does not build: libc 0.2.191 has no proc_pidinfo for iOS","message":"The nightly release's iOS runtimes job failed on 2026-11-23 (run 45154415848): ash_std did not compile for aarch64-apple-ios-sim, with \"cannot find type proc_taskinfo / function proc_pidinfo / value PROC_PIDTASKINFO in crate libc\" at std/src/sys.rs hlp_sys_process_memory.\n\nCause: the beadie 0.4.0 move (49d6e40c) rewrote Cargo.lock and took libc from 0.2.189 to 0.2.191, which provides the libproc calls on macOS only. The code, from August, was cfg'd for macos and ios. libproc is not part of the iOS SDK. Nothing on a push builds the iOS runtime, and the ios-assets job is continue-on-error, so the failure showed only as a failed job in a nightly run that still published.\n\nFix: iOS reads the Mach task's resident size (task_info with MACH_TASK_BASIC_INFO), which the iOS SDK has; macOS keeps proc_pidinfo. Checked by building ash_std for aarch64-apple-ios-sim, aarch64-apple-ios and x86_64-apple-ios, and by running the same task_info call on macOS (resident size rises by the 64 MB touched).\n\nOpen: nothing builds the iOS runtime on a push, so the next dependency bump can break it the same way. A cargo check of ash_std for an iOS triple in the lint workflow would catch it.","files":null}]}